<hr data-start="241" data-end="244" class="">
<h3 data-start="246" data-end="344" class=""><strong data-start="250" data-end="344">The TARTAN Framework as a Categorical Encoding System for Embodied and Semantic Perception</strong></h3>
<p data-start="346" data-end="932" class="">The TARTAN (Trajectory-Aware Recursive Tiling with Annotated Noise) system provides a formal architecture for compositional scene understanding through layered representational structures. At its core, TARTAN partitions the perceptual manifold into three interacting strata: the <strong data-start="625" data-end="643">semantic layer</strong>, the <strong data-start="649" data-end="667">physical layer</strong>, and the <strong data-start="677" data-end="705">holographic tartan layer</strong>. These layers may be understood as fibered categories over a shared base space of observed spatiotemporal events, enabling a coherent compositional mapping between symbolic intent, embodied dynamics, and reconstructive memory.</p>
<hr data-start="934" data-end="937" class="">
<h3 data-start="939" data-end="997" class=""><strong data-start="943" data-end="997">1. Semantic Layer: Ontological Color-Fiber Functor</strong></h3>
<p data-start="999" data-end="1224" class="">Let us define a base perceptual category <strong data-start="1040" data-end="1046">ùí™</strong>, whose objects are localized spatiotemporal observations and whose morphisms are perceptual transitions or sensorimotor affordances. The <strong data-start="1184" data-end="1202">semantic layer</strong> constructs a functor:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">S</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant="script">O</mi><mo>‚Üí</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="script">C</mi><mtext>Type</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}: \mathcal{O} \rightarrow \mathcal{C}_{\text{Type}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.075em;">S</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">:</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.02778em;">O</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">‚Üí</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.9694em; vertical-align: -0.2861em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.05834em;">C</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0583em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord text mtight"><span class="mord mtight">Type</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">‚Äã</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2861em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="1296" data-end="1624" class="">where <strong data-start="1302" data-end="1312">ùíû‚Çú·µß‚Çö‚Çë</strong> is a semantic vector space modeled as a <strong data-start="1353" data-end="1384">3D color wheel fiber bundle</strong>, where each fiber <strong data-start="1403" data-end="1410">ùíû‚Çì</strong> over an observation <strong data-start="1431" data-end="1445">x ‚àà Ob(ùí™)</strong> encodes the ontological class (e.g., real horse vs balloon vs robot horse), stylistic encoding (e.g., painting vs photo), and epistemic modulation (e.g., ergodicity of evidence).</p>
<p data-start="1626" data-end="1665" class="">Each object <strong data-start="1638" data-end="1643">x</strong> is lifted to a tuple:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">S</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>Œ≤</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>œµ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>‚àà</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="double-struck">S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>√ó</mo><mi mathvariant="script">T</mi><mo>√ó</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="double-struck">R</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(x) = (h, \beta, \epsilon) \in \mathbb{S}^2 \times \mathcal{T} \times \mathbb{R}^+</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.075em;">S</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">h</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05278em;">Œ≤</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">œµ</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">‚àà</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.9474em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathbb">S</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8641em;"><span style="top: -3.113em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">√ó</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7667em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.25417em;">T</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">√ó</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8213em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathbb">R</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8213em;"><span style="top: -3.113em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mbin mtight">+</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>
<ul data-start="1768" data-end="2153">
<li data-start="1768" data-end="1881" class="">
<p data-start="1770" data-end="1881" class=""><strong data-start="1770" data-end="1780">h ‚àà ‚Ñù¬≥</strong> (Hue vector on the unit color wheel): encodes category membership in a compact Lie group structure</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1882" data-end="2038" class="">
<p data-start="1884" data-end="2038" class=""><strong data-start="1884" data-end="1894">Œ≤ ‚àà ùíØ</strong> (Blink template): maps source modality to a temporal frequency filter, such as flicker for paintings or interlaced scanlines for analog video</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2039" data-end="2153" class="">
<p data-start="2041" data-end="2153" class=""><strong data-start="2041" data-end="2051">Œµ ‚àà ‚Ñù‚Å∫</strong> (Ergodicity width): inversely proportional to interpolation uncertainty, approximated by aura breadth</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2155" data-end="2333" class="">This functor is <strong data-start="2171" data-end="2184">covariant</strong> with respect to sensor transformations; that is, changes in viewpoint or sensor domain preserve type identity but may alter blink rate or certainty.</p>
<hr data-start="2335" data-end="2338" class="">
<h3 data-start="2340" data-end="2411" class=""><strong data-start="2344" data-end="2411">2. Physical Layer: Aura Field as a Sheaf of Physical Properties</strong></h3>
<p data-start="2413" data-end="2618" class="">The physical layer is treated as a <strong data-start="2448" data-end="2476">sheaf of physical fields</strong> over the observation manifold. For each open region <strong data-start="2529" data-end="2538">U ‚äÜ ‚Ñ≥</strong>, where ‚Ñ≥ is the 4D spatiotemporal manifold, we define a sheaf <strong data-start="2601" data-end="2607">ùîΩ</strong> such that:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>ùîΩ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>œÅ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">‚à£</mi><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>‚àà</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ùîΩ(U) = \{ (œÅ(x), T(x), v(x)) \ |\ x ‚àà U \}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathbb">F</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.10903em;">U</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">{(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">œÅ</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">v</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mclose">))</span><span class="mspace">&nbsp;</span><span class="mord">‚à£</span><span class="mspace">&nbsp;</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">‚àà</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.10903em;">U</span><span class="mclose">}</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="2671" data-end="2676" class="">Here:</p>
<ul data-start="2677" data-end="2817">
<li data-start="2677" data-end="2717" class="">
<p data-start="2679" data-end="2717" class=""><strong data-start="2679" data-end="2687">œÅ(x)</strong> is the local density function</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2718" data-end="2753" class="">
<p data-start="2720" data-end="2753" class=""><strong data-start="2720" data-end="2728">T(x)</strong> is the local temperature</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2754" data-end="2817" class="">
<p data-start="2756" data-end="2817" class=""><strong data-start="2756" data-end="2764">v(x)</strong> is the local velocity vector or worldline projection</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2819" data-end="3052" class="">This sheaf structure ensures local consistency and global composability: any intersection of overlapping observations yields physically compatible subfields, enabling reconstruction of continuous dynamics across partial observations.</p>
<p data-start="3054" data-end="3096" class="">Pixel-stretching acts as a projection map:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>œÄ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant="script">I</mi><mo>‚Üí</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="script">I</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">‚Ä≤</mo></msup><mspace width="1em"></mspace><mtext>such&nbsp;that</mtext><mspace width="1em"></mspace><msub><mi>œÄ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mo>‚à´</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>œÑ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>œÑ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_t : \mathcal{I} \rightarrow \mathcal{I}' \quad \text{such that} \quad \pi_t(x) = x + \int_0^t v(x(\tau)) d\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5806em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">œÄ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2806em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0359em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">t</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">‚Äã</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">:</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.07382em;">I</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">‚Üí</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.0519em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.07382em;">I</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8019em;"><span style="top: -3.113em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mtight">‚Ä≤</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 1em;"></span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">such&nbsp;that</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 1em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">œÄ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2806em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0359em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">t</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">‚Äã</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6667em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">+</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 2.4554em; vertical-align: -0.9119em;"></span><span class="mop"><span class="mop op-symbol large-op" style="margin-right: 0.44445em; position: relative; top: -0.0011em;">‚à´</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 1.5435em;"><span style="top: -1.7881em; margin-left: -0.4445em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">0</span></span></span><span style="top: -3.8129em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">t</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">‚Äã</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.9119em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">v</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.1132em;">œÑ</span><span class="mclose">))</span><span class="mord mathnormal">d</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.1132em;">œÑ</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="3220" data-end="3347" class="">which embeds the trajectory integral into pixel coordinates, thereby allowing motion fields to be rendered as aura distortions.</p>
<hr data-start="3349" data-end="3352" class="">
<h3 data-start="3354" data-end="3443" class=""><strong data-start="3358" data-end="3443">3. Holographic Tartan Layer: Recursive Functorial Tiling and Evidence Propagation</strong></h3>
<p data-start="3445" data-end="3677" class="">The third layer constructs a recursive tiling of the scene through <strong data-start="3512" data-end="3543">functorial scene repetition</strong> and <strong data-start="3548" data-end="3568">semantic caching</strong>, borrowing concepts from both holography and topological data analysis. Define a recursive covering functor:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">R</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant="script">O</mi><mo>‚Üí</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="script">T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">‚àû</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}: \mathcal{O} \rightarrow \mathcal{T}^\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">R</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">:</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.02778em;">O</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">‚Üí</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7144em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.25417em;">T</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.7144em;"><span style="top: -3.113em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">‚àû</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="3742" data-end="3845" class="">where <strong data-start="3748" data-end="3756">ùíØ^‚àû</strong> is an infinite tower of tiling domains across scales and angles. This functor satisfies:</p>
<ul data-start="3847" data-end="4136">
<li data-start="3847" data-end="4011" class="">
<p data-start="3849" data-end="4011" class=""><strong data-start="3849" data-end="3868">Self-similarity</strong>: for any subscene <strong data-start="3887" data-end="3892">s</strong>, there exists a tiling morphism <strong data-start="3925" data-end="3940">f: s ‚Üí R(s)</strong>, where <strong data-start="3948" data-end="3956">R(s)</strong> is a scaled or transformed copy with embedded metadata</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4012" data-end="4136" class="">
<p data-start="4014" data-end="4136" class=""><strong data-start="4014" data-end="4035">Cross-consistency</strong>: overlapping tilings propagate evidence across scenes, enabling inference from one region to another</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="4138" data-end="4383" class="">This layer incorporates steganographic overlays for embedding provenance and context, forming a <strong data-start="4234" data-end="4263">bundle of evidence fields</strong>. These overlays are not merely decorative; they carry indexical metadata, functional affordances, and narrative priors.</p>
<hr data-start="4385" data-end="4388" class="">
<h3 data-start="4390" data-end="4447" class=""><strong data-start="4394" data-end="4447">Compositional Semantics and Cross-Layer Coherence</strong></h3>
<p data-start="4449" data-end="4659" class="">Crucially, the three layers do not operate in isolation but are <strong data-start="4513" data-end="4537">functorially aligned</strong> over the base category <strong data-start="4561" data-end="4567">ùí™</strong>. That is, there exists a natural transformation between the semantic and physical functors:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>Œ∑</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant="script">S</mi><mo>‚áí</mo><mi mathvariant="script">F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta: \mathcal{S} \Rightarrow \mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">Œ∑</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">:</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.075em;">S</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">‚áí</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.09931em;">F</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="4710" data-end="4948" class="">which encodes how symbolic distinctions (e.g., 'balloon horse') map to physical expectations (e.g., low density, slow deformation velocity). Similarly, the recursive tiling functor acts as a higher-order endofunctor over scene categories:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">R</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">S</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">c</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">e</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">e</mi></mrow><mo>‚Üí</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">S</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">c</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">e</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}: \mathbf{Scene} \rightarrow \mathbf{Scene}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">R</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">:</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6861em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathbf">Scene</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">‚Üí</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6861em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathbf">Scene</span></span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="5012" data-end="5119" class="">endowing the system with the ability to both compress and reconstruct scenes through structured repetition.</p>
<hr data-start="5121" data-end="5124" class="">
<h3 data-start="5126" data-end="5144" class=""><strong data-start="5130" data-end="5144">Conclusion</strong></h3>
<p data-start="5146" data-end="5723" class="">The TARTAN architecture thus defines a <strong data-start="5185" data-end="5222">tri-layered categorical hierarchy</strong>, with semantic, physical, and structural redundancy layers interwoven via functorial and sheaf-theoretic constructs. The <strong data-start="5344" data-end="5381">color-wheel semantic vector space</strong> acts as a fibered differentiator atop a dense field of physical motion and is recursively propagated by a holographic tiling regime. This approach ensures interpretability, compositionality, and physics-constrained generativity‚Äîaddressing the entanglement and redundancy pathologies identified in emergent systems such as the FER hypothesis.</p>
