<h3 data-start="3455" data-end="3492" class=""><strong data-start="3459" data-end="3492">Detailed Thematic Comparisons</strong></h3>
<h4 data-start="3494" data-end="3576" class=""><strong data-start="3499" data-end="3576">1. Semantic Differentiation (Color Wheel Fibering vs Latent Coactivation)</strong></h4>
<p data-start="3577" data-end="4070" class="">TARTAN introduces a <strong data-start="3597" data-end="3658">functor from observations to a semantically fibered space</strong>, explicitly encoding ontological type, style, and confidence via radial modulation (color vector + blink template + aura width). By contrast, ViT and NeRF conflate appearance with identity, requiring external decoders to distinguish between materially different but visually similar instances (e.g., robot horse vs real horse). FER systems lack typed fibers entirely; all semantics are emergent from statistics.</p>
<h4 data-start="4072" data-end="4139" class=""><strong data-start="4077" data-end="4139">2. Physical Realism (Aura Sheaves vs Interpolation Fields)</strong></h4>
<p data-start="4140" data-end="4526" class="">In TARTAN, <strong data-start="4151" data-end="4175">Gaussian aura fields</strong> encode physical observables as sections of a sheaf over the perceptual manifold, with time-integrated velocity encoded through pixel deformation. This allows physical reasoning (e.g., causality, pressure, inertia) directly from the visual field. NeRF variants interpolate density without thermodynamic coherence; velocity must be externally inferred.</p>
<h4 data-start="4528" data-end="4602" class=""><strong data-start="4533" data-end="4602">3. Temporal Dynamics (Worldline Embedding vs Attention Sequences)</strong></h4>
<p data-start="4603" data-end="4991" class="">TARTAN encodes <strong data-start="4618" data-end="4663">worldlines as deformed pixel trajectories</strong> directly within the scene, allowing reconstruction of dynamic flow. Transformer systems, by contrast, use self-attention across discrete time slices, lacking grounded spatiotemporal continuity. Active Inference captures this more naturally but lacks the visual representational efficiency and parallelizable encoding of TARTAN.</p>
<h4 data-start="4993" data-end="5039" class=""><strong data-start="4998" data-end="5039">4. Recursive and Compositional Tiling</strong></h4>
<p data-start="5040" data-end="5420" class="">The <strong data-start="5044" data-end="5075">recursive functorial tiling</strong> in TARTAN (inspired by holography and renormalization groups) stands apart from traditional models: it enables the repetition and propagation of evidence across scales and scenes, much like fractal memory or holographic inference. This is absent in NeRFs, FER, or ViTs, where scenes are entangled but not self-similar in a reconstructive sense.</p>
<h4 data-start="5422" data-end="5461" class=""><strong data-start="5427" data-end="5461">5. Auditability and Provenance</strong></h4>
<p data-start="5462" data-end="5840" class="">TARTAN employs <strong data-start="5477" data-end="5504">steganographic overlays</strong> and evidence bundles to track provenance, ergodicity, and inter-sensor agreement. This enables <strong data-start="5600" data-end="5626">epistemic auditability</strong>, unlike latent-only models. Transformer-based vision systems offer partial interpretability through attention maps, but no architectural layer is dedicated to epistemic tracking or multiscale evidence propagation.</p>
<hr data-start="5842" data-end="5845" class="">
<h3 data-start="5847" data-end="5905" class=""><strong data-start="5851" data-end="5905">Conclusion: Toward a Physics-First Semantic Vision</strong></h3>
<p data-start="5907" data-end="6428" class="">The TARTAN framework diverges from existing paradigms by proposing a <strong data-start="5976" data-end="6063">mathematically principled, physically grounded, and semantically typed architecture</strong>. It embodies a shift from data-maximalism and opaque embeddings toward <strong data-start="6135" data-end="6169">structured functorial mappings</strong> between symbol, sensation, and space. Rather than replacing existing models, it may serve as a <strong data-start="6265" data-end="6285">generative spine</strong>‚Äîa middleware layer integrating raw perceptual input, symbolic annotation, and dynamic reconstruction in a tractable and interpretable fashion.</p>
